  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>A) Number of values of -n=1,2,3....<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8734;</mo></math></p><p>B) Number of values of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>to</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>C) Number of values of m=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>to</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></math></p><p>Total values=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>D)The two possible orientations of electronic spin, which are spin quantum numbers are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>E) For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math> total number of orbital is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></math></p>"
